<%#
  Rendered into the session card body by the dashboard.

  Unlike the Jupyter apps, this deliberately does NOT report the image. That is
  fixed by submit.yml.erb rather than chosen on the form, so it is identical on
  every session of this app and printing it would just be a constant taking up
  the card.

  The GPU request is the one launch choice that changes what the session can
  actually do, and nothing else on the card reveals it -- the dashboard shows
  host, times, and session id, and the app title is fixed by manifest.yml.

  Shown only when a GPU was asked for. A "GPU: no" line on every CPU-only
  session is noise, and its absence is not ambiguous once users know the card
  calls out GPUs and stays quiet otherwise -- which matches the Jupyter apps,
  so the two read the same way side by side.

  ERB binding here is the BatchConnect::Session itself, which is where
  user_context comes from: the launch form values, read back from
  user_defined_context.json in the session's staged directory. Keys are form
  attribute ids, so this must track form.yml's `gpus`.

  to_i rather than a truthiness test: the checkbox arrives as the string "1" or
  "0" (form.yml's checked_value/unchecked_value), and "0" is truthy in Ruby. It
  also copes with the key being absent on sessions staged before this field
  existed, and makes the value safe to interpolate without escaping.
-%>
<%- gpus = user_context["gpus"].to_i -%>
<%- if gpus > 0 -%>
**GPU<%= 's' if gpus > 1 %>:** <%= gpus %> &times; `nvidia.com/gpu`
<%- end -%>
